# Complementary Filter Debugging, Drift Analysis, and Real-Time IMU Validation

## Overview

Today focused on one of the most important phases of the project so far: diagnosing and correcting inertial estimation errors inside the complementary filter pipeline.

Rather than simply implementing new functionality, the session evolved into a deep systems-debugging and validation exercise involving:

* accelerometer reference correction
* gyro drift analysis
* real-time timing verification
* sensor fusion stabilization
* long-duration telemetry validation

This phase represents a major transition from “building features” into true embedded systems engineering and estimation debugging.

---

## Major Problem Encountered — Ghost Tilt

During long-duration flat-surface testing, the fused orientation estimate slowly drifted upward even while the IMU remained stationary.

Initial observations showed:

* gyro angle continuously climbing over time
* fused angle slowly being pulled away from true flat orientation
* accelerometer pitch producing incorrect reference behavior during early testing

This created a “ghost tilt” phenomenon where the system falsely believed the sensor was slowly rotating despite remaining physically still.

---

## Root Cause Investigation

### Accelerometer Reference Failure

A critical issue was identified in the accelerometer preprocessing pipeline.

Originally:

```cpp
corrected_az = sample.az - az_offset;
```

This unintentionally removed the gravity vector from the Z-axis before pitch computation.

Because pitch estimation depends on the geometry of the gravity vector, subtracting the full gravity magnitude caused:

* incorrect pitch reference geometry
* false tilt estimates
* severe fusion instability

This explained the earlier 40° false pitch behavior while the sensor was physically flat.

---

## Accelerometer Reference Fix

The correction involved preserving the Z-axis gravity reference during pitch estimation:

```cpp
corrected_az = sample.az;
```

while still allowing minor X/Y bias corrections.

After the fix:

* flat pitch estimates dropped from ~40° to ~0.6–0.7°
* fused angle stabilized dramatically
* orientation estimates became physically meaningful

This represented a major conceptual breakthrough regarding accelerometer geometry and gravity-vector-based orientation estimation.

---

## Timing and Integration Improvements

Another major issue identified was fixed-time integration.

Originally:

```cpp
const float dt = 0.10;
```

This assumed perfect 100ms loop execution timing.

The system was updated to compute real elapsed time dynamically using:

```cpp
float dt = (now - lastSample) / 1000.0;
```

This improved:

* gyro integration accuracy
* complementary filter consistency
* long-duration drift behavior

This reinforced an important embedded systems concept:
real-time systems should not assume ideal timing behavior.

---

## Gyroscope Drift Analysis

Long-duration stationary testing revealed:

* gyro integration still accumulated slow drift
* residual gyro bias remained even after initial calibration
* filtered gyro values remained slightly positive during flat tests

Through telemetry analysis:

* residual drift rates were estimated
* bias trends were measured directly from logged data
* gyro offset tuning became data-driven rather than guess-based

This highlighted a major inertial systems reality:

Even very small gyro biases accumulate into large orientation errors over long periods of integration.

---

## Complementary Filter Validation

Despite gyro drift, the complementary filter demonstrated strong stabilization behavior.

Observed behavior:

* pure gyro angle continuously climbed
* accelerometer pitch remained relatively stable
* fused angle remained bounded and physically reasonable

This validated the core purpose of the complementary filter:

* gyro provides smooth short-term motion tracking
* accelerometer provides long-term orientation correction
* fusion combines both strengths while minimizing individual weaknesses

The system successfully resisted catastrophic orientation divergence despite imperfect gyro calibration.

---

